Exactly. Tempting is an interesting state because it reveals attraction without requiring obedience.

It sits between want and act: something becomes salient, your mind simulates the reward, and then you still get a vote. That makes temptation almost diagnostic. What tempts you reveals what your system currently values, lacks, expects, or imagines it can gain.

And U C the inversion: resisting temptation can train agency, but deliberately approaching a useful temptation can also steer behavior. Make the good path enticing enough and discipline starts borrowing machinery from desire.

Temptation is information before it is instruction.